# ⚠⚠ REDACTED COPY — this is the file that is PUBLIC.
#
# Two values are placeholders and must be filled in for a real deployment:
#   sharegate=YOUR_ACCESS_CODE_HERE   the gate code, compared against the visitor's cookie
#   YOUR.SERVER.IP                    the host
#
# ⛔ The working copy with the real values lives beside it as `Caddyfile.share`, which is
# gitignored. Do not "sync" this file back over that one, and never paste a real code in here.
#
# SHARE — the vhost for sharepons.family
#
# ⛔⛔⛔ THIS BOX SERVES ~31 OTHER SITES. THIS FILE IS ADDITIVE AND NOTHING ELSE.
#
# Append this block to the server's Caddyfile. Do not reformat, reorder, or "tidy" the rest of that
# file while you are in there: a diff that touches another project's vhost is a diff that can take
# that project down, and every site on this box shares one Caddy process.
#
# ⛔⛔ NEVER RUN `caddy stop`. It ignores `--config`, talks to the admin API on :2019, and stops the
# SYSTEMD instance — which is every vhost on the box. It exits 0 and looks deliberate. It has taken
# all 31 sites down on this stack once already. To apply this:
#
#     # 1. back the live config up FIRST, so a bad edit is one command from undone
#     ssh $HOST 'cp /etc/caddy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.bak-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)'
#     # 2. append this block
#     # 3. check it PARSES before asking Caddy to load it
#     ssh $HOST 'caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile'
#     # 4. reload — never stop/start
#     ssh $HOST 'systemctl reload caddy'
#     # 5. prove the OTHER sites still answer, not just this one
#     for d in pump.family rhcompany.xyz tagonpons.xyz stakepons.xyz; do
#       printf '%-20s ' "$d"; curl -sI -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' "https://$d/"
#     done
#
# ## ⚠ Certificates
#
# DNS was set 4 Sep 2026: apex and www → YOUR.SERVER.IP, and **no AAAA records**, which is what makes
# this straightforward. A parking-page import brings AAAA records along, Let's Encrypt prefers IPv6,
# and issuance then fails against a host that is not listening there — silently, while every A
# record looks perfect. Re-check with `dig +short @dns1.registrar-servers.com sharepons.family AAAA`
# before blaming Caddy. A record that is PROXIED can never get a cert either; the tell is HTTP 525.

# ## ✅ THE SITE IS PUBLIC — the gate was removed 5 Sep 2026
#
# It ran for one day: HTTP basic auth on 4 Sep, then a designed gate page, then nothing. The history
# matters only for one reason — **the gate is restorable and the parts are still here.** The page
# still lives in `gate/` in the repo and at /root/share-gate on the box, and the matcher and handler
# that enforced it are in git and in /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.bak-shareopen-*. See the block inside the
# site definition for exactly how to put it back and what must sit above it.
#
# ⛔ THE ACCESS CODE NO LONGER GRANTS ANYTHING. `?key=` writes a cookie nothing reads.
#
# ⛔⛔ THIS HAS NOTHING TO DO WITH THE FIRST-LAUNCH GATE, which is in the bundle and is still up
# until `ShareLaunchpad.count() > 0`. Opening the site did not open that, and closing the site again
# would not close it. @see web/src/lib/launchGate.ts, docs/first-launch-gate.md.
#
# To redeploy the gate page (only meaningful if the gate comes back):
#
#     rsync -az --delete gate/ root@YOUR.SERVER.IP:/root/share-gate/
#
# ⛔ `--delete` here is safe ONLY because /root/share-gate holds nothing but the gate. Never point
# it at a parent, and never at /root/share-web (its parent is not the site root — dist is).

sharepons.family, www.sharepons.family {
	encode zstd gzip

	# ⛔⛔ www MUST REDIRECT TO THE APEX, AND IT IS THE GATE THAT MAKES IT LOAD-BEARING.
	# The gate page sets `sharegate` with NO Domain attribute, so the cookie is HOST-ONLY: a cookie
	# written on sharepons.family is never sent to www.sharepons.family. Both names resolve to this
	# box and both were served by this same block, so anybody who reached the site via www was gated
	# PERMANENTLY — the ?key= link would set a cookie on the apex and then hand them straight back to
	# the gate on www, with no error and nothing in the page to explain it. Reported as "the preview
	# link doesn't work", 5 Sep 2026.
	# ⚠ Fix it HERE, not by adding `domain=.sharepons.family` to the cookie: widening a credential
	# cookie to every subdomain is the wrong direction, and one canonical host is worth having anyway.
	# ⭐ The redirect keeps {uri}, so the ?key= query survives it and the link works on either host.
	@www host www.sharepons.family
	redir @www https://sharepons.family{uri} permanent

	# ══ THE GATE IS GONE — THE SITE IS PUBLIC (5 Sep 2026) ═════════════════════════════════════
	# The private-preview gate that lived here has been REMOVED at the operator's instruction. It was
	# a cookie (`sharegate`) compared by Caddy over the whole domain, served from /root/share-gate.
	#
	# ⭐ WHAT IS LEFT BEHIND, AND WHY IT IS LEFT: /root/share-gate is still ON THE BOX, untouched, and
	# the full block is one `git show` away (it was removed in the commit that opened the site; the
	# live config before this change is /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.bak-shareopen-*). Restoring the gate is
	# re-adding a matcher and a handle — nothing about it was one-way.
	#
	# ⛔⛔ THE ACCESS CODE IS NOW MEANINGLESS. `?key=` sets a cookie nothing compares. Do not hand that
	# link to anybody as though it still grants something, and do not treat it as a credential that
	# still needs protecting — the thing it opened no longer exists.
	#
	# ⛔⛔ OPENING THE SITE DID NOT OPEN THE FIRST-LAUNCH GATE, AND THE TWO WERE NEVER RELATED. That
	# one is in the BUNDLE (web/src/lib/launchGate.ts): until `ShareLaunchpad.count() > 0`, the launch
	# form refuses anybody but the first-launch wallet, and it opens by itself the moment token #1
	# exists. Nothing in this file has ever touched it. @see docs/first-launch-gate.md.
	# ⚠ But note what opening the site DOES change about it: that gate is an INTERFACE gate over a
	# launchpad with no owner and no admin, so the window in which a stranger can call `launch()`
	# directly and take token #1 is now a window the whole internet can see. It was always bypassable
	# by anyone reading the public repo; it is now bypassable by anyone reading the site. ➤ Launch.
	#
	# ⭐⭐ /privacy, /terms AND /data-deletion ARE NOW SERVED BY THE APP, NOT AS FLAT HTML. They are
	# real routes in the SPA router, so the `handle` at the bottom answers them via /index.html. The
	# prerendered copies in /root/share-gate existed ONLY so App Review could read a policy through a
	# closed gate; with the gate gone that reason is spent, and the real pages carry the site's nav.
	# ⚠ The one thing lost: those copies were flat HTML with zero JavaScript, and these are not. If
	# Meta's tooling ever fails to read the policy, that is the cause — the fix is to copy
	# gate/{privacy,terms,data-deletion}.html into the dist root and add `{path}.html` to `try_files`
	# below, NOT to gate the site again. ⛔ Re-run `npm run legal:static` first; a stale policy is
	# worse than a JavaScript one.

	# ══ INSTAGRAM'S DEAUTHORIZE CALLBACK ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
	# ⚠ THIS IS NOW REDUNDANT — `handle /api/*` below routes it identically — AND IT IS KEPT ANYWAY,
	# because the reason it exists is not obvious from the outside and rediscovering it cost a
	# debugging session:
	#
	# Instagram's deauthorize callback is a server-to-server POST from Meta's machines. Behind a
	# static-file gate it does not merely fail — `file_server` REFUSES POST, so Meta receives 405
	# Method Not Allowed, which is not a "your app is private" answer, it is a broken endpoint.
	#
	# ⛔⛔ SO IF A GATE IS EVER PUT BACK, THIS BLOCK MUST SIT ABOVE IT. Caddy takes the first matching
	# handle in a mutually-exclusive group; below the gate matcher it never runs.
	# ⚠ And it is safe above one, unlike a page route: it reverse-proxies to the API, answers only
	# JSON, requires a valid HMAC over Meta's signed_request, and returns 400 to everything else.
	# There is no bundle behind it and nothing to navigate to. Exempting a PAGE route would have
	# opened the whole site, because index.html pulls the entire application.
	# @see server/src/index.ts and server/src/providers/metaSignedRequest.ts
	handle /api/instagram/deauthorize {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5234
	}

	# ⛔⛔ THE API MUST BE MATCHED BEFORE THE SPA FALLBACK. Ordering is the whole thing: reversed,
	# `try_files` answers /api/* with index.html, `/api/providers` parses as nothing, and the claim
	# page renders "no platform is wired up" on a deployment where every platform is.
	handle /api/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5234
	}

	# ⛔⛔ TOKEN LOGOS ARE SERVED FROM OUTSIDE THE SITE ROOT, ON PURPOSE. A token's `logo` is written
	# into its constructor with NO SETTER on Pons V2, so the URL is permanent. Inside the deploy root
	# the web deploy's `rsync --delete` would erase every launched token's logo, forever.
	handle /logos/* {
		reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:5234
	}

	handle {
		root * /root/share-web/dist
		# ⛔⛔ NOT OPTIONAL. Without the fallback every link works inside the app while every REFRESH
		# and every shared link 404s — the failure nobody sees while clicking around.
		try_files {path} /index.html
		file_server
	}

	header {
		# ⚠ Live on the box and easy to lose on a resync: Caddy names itself in every response
		# otherwise. Not a control, just one less free fact about what is running here.
		-Server
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
	}

	# ⛔⛔ ANY RESPONSE WHOSE BODY DEPENDS ON A REQUEST HEADER MUST SAY SO. Added 5 Sep 2026 after the
	# preview gate made `/` two different documents at one URL — 11KB gate page without the cookie,
	# 3KB app shell with it — while the response advertised only `Vary: Accept-Encoding`. Browsers
	# that had seen the gate cached THAT for `/`, so setting the cookie and calling replace('/') was
	# answered out of local disk with the gate again, never reaching the server: a working door that
	# looked completely dead. Reported as "that preview link dont work".
	#
	# ⭐ THE GATE IS GONE AND THIS STAYS, for two reasons that outlive it:
	#   1. `/api/*` genuinely varies by the SESSION cookie — /api/session and the claim endpoints
	#      answer differently per signed-in visitor, and that must never land in a shared cache.
	#   2. index.html is the SPA's entry point and names the content-hashed bundle. Cached, a
	#      visitor keeps loading the PREVIOUS deploy's asset names after every redeploy — which is
	#      exactly what happens the moment the token's CA is baked in and shipped.
	#
	# ⛔ It must NOT cover /assets/* or /logos/* — those filenames carry a content hash or a token
	# id, never change under the same name, and are the only things here worth a long cache.
	# ⚠ `+Vary` APPENDS. A bare `Vary` would replace the `Accept-Encoding` that `encode` sets, and
	# the reward for that is a gzip body served to a client that asked for identity.
	@cookie-dependent not path /assets/* /logos/*
	header @cookie-dependent {
		+Vary Cookie
		Cache-Control "no-store"
	}

	# ⚠ og.png must answer as an IMAGE. A missing one served by the SPA fallback answers 200
	# text/html — a blank X card that every og validator passes:
	#     curl -sI https://sharepons.family/og.png | grep -i content-type
}
